A||||7||MontanaFiero||To strike or Not to srike...||||2||
Z||000000||MontanaFiero||03-10-2001||04:16 AM||||Well, here I go with another controversy. Read this article: <A HREF="http://www.Billingsgazette.com/index.php?section=business&display=content/business/1northwest.inc" TARGET=_blank>http://www.Billingsgazette.com/index.php?section=business&display=content/bus iness/1northwest.inc</A> <BR>Then let me know what you all think.<BR>.   I am a union man, myself. Long ago congress gave workers the right to join unions and the right to strike if that's what it took to prove your point. The reason this was done originally was to keep powerful, slave driving companies from stripping workers of all their rights and making them work for almost non-existent wages.<BR>.   President Reagan took that right away from union government workers (air traffic controllers and postal workers specifically), but as far as I know the civilian unions and workers still have the right to strike. When I was hired on to the Postal Service I was told I do not have that right-- so that's ok. I was informed. <BR>.   Now Bush has done the same thing to plain old civilian workers. What  about these guys? Now the airlines (huge powerful corporations) won't give the workers anything, in fact they might just lower their wages because they know the feds will just step in and prevent any union action.<BR>.   On the other hand I will still be able to catch my flight out of here on the 26th...<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/blee291/fiero/mtfieroplate.jpg">    <BR><p>[This message has been edited by MontanaFiero (edited 03-10-2001).]||||reg||2
Z||000001||Old Lar||03-10-2001||08:58 AM||||There are many way to have almost the same effect as a strike.  Sick ins, work slow downs, especially with the airlines and some of the questionable safety records.  Start doing the job by the book.  If I had to follow the "book" the time it would take to do the "job" would double or triple.<P>What I see is that when wages go up say 5%(strike or not)costs go up 7%.  You never get ahead.<P>I have been working for 35+ years.  My salary has gone up 7-8 times in dollar amount (a lot more responsibilities added on) but my buying power has not kept up at all.<P>With a strike, you lose your income while out and unless you get a giant raise in salary, you never make up what you lost when you are out.  Plus those that needed your service lose out on that service, then pay a higher premium when the work starts back up.<P>A no win situation for everyone.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||03-10-2001||12:10 PM||||The true power of a strike in todays economy is very small.<P>I was on the "management" side of the line in a strike by IBEW in the early 90's. Those guys stayed out around 3 months. meanwhile "management", which was everyone but the so called managers in most cases, went out to the sites and ran bussiness as usual. Most people not even know there was a strike. The only people who really lost anything were the rank & file union guys. By the time "management" got done with expense reports we actually came out ahead.<P>I used to live by New Process Gear, a Chrysler transmission plant in Syracuse. Those guys went on strike in the 80's. NPG actually stayed open because of contracts with Ford and GM. Again the only people who lost anything were the union guys that didn't work on the chrysler lines. The lines making transfer cases for ford etc, stayed in operation.<P>Strikes usually have little impact on the company. They just declair it a tax loss and move on. With the number of contracts comming up in the airline industry, there's no way Bush is going to let them strike if it can be stopped. I think you'll find the cutting of the Ergo sections from OSHA reg's is only the begining of the anti union items comming.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||03-10-2001||02:07 PM||||Unions were a neccessity when they came into being. Times have changed but most unions seem to have failed to recognize this. OSHA (and the court system) have made striking over real safety issues virtually a thing of the past. The mobility of the modern workforce means most companies have to keep their wages reasonably in line. The days of striking for a 50% wage increase are also gone. Going on strike for 6 months to get ten cents an hour is insane. You aren't going to live long enough to break even. Even worse, because the company is often better equipped to hold out than the union, the workers often wind up settling for LESS than the original offer.<P><p>[This message has been edited by DRH (edited 03-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||deceler8||03-10-2001||05:34 PM||||I think President Bush acted as he did because the potential exists for overlapping strikes at more than one airline. <P>I believe American might also strike this summer.<P>I can't fault Mr. Bush for acting. Air travel has been so screwed up for so long that to not act IMO would have been worse.<P>Reagan was right to fire the air traffic controllers. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||gixxer||03-10-2001||06:30 PM||||Good old Bush gave them the option to stay with their job, or vote with their feet.<BR>Being in the airplane business, I would guess that a strike by those mechanics would have a very immediate and costly impact on the airline.<BR>A strike by the engineers and techs association (SPEEA) here at Boeing immediatly halted the delivery of airplanes, and the production lines halted entirely within a week or so.<BR>We also enjoyed the full support of the production workers (IAM), who did their best(worst) to slow production. Of course the airlines were furious.<BR>This was a nice 40 day vacation for me.<BR>Apparently the CEO of our company was under immense pressure from OTHER companies not to cave in to our demands, which probably explains some of the length of the strike.<BR>My lost wages were partly made up by bonuses for getting production back up to speed.<BR>Since a company IS people, no people coming to work generally would mean; no company.<BR>But I suppose that depends what the company does....  <BR>I think Reagan was a senile pig for firing the ATCers.<BR>Oink to you Reagan.<BR>And a big Washinton OINK! to Pigmaster Bush.    ||||reg||1
Z||000006||MontanaFiero||03-11-2001||04:28 AM||||Isn't that the whole idea in the first place... to shut a company down and make them realize just how important the workers really are? The ideal situation would be having the <I>threat</I> of a strike be enough of a concern to a company like an airline to make them bargain in good faith. But then enter Mr. Bush with an executive order. As far as I can tell, all this is just more government control.  I'm all for settling things the easy way, but the company I work for didn't even show up to the bargaining table just because they know we can't strike. (The postal service, being federal is forbidden from striking). We have been working <I>without</I> a contract since november.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||BN Boomer||03-12-2001||08:48 PM||||First off, let me preface this by stating that I am a pretty staunch conservative and vote almost straight Republican, including for GW Bush. But their hypocracy on this issue really bothers me. On one hand, they say that the government shouldn't be regulating business too heavily, yet on the other hand they turn around and jump right smack in the middle of labor negotiations like this. And it's not just the Republicans. Clinton gave us a pretty good screwing over, too. Mr "Friend of labor", "I feel your pain" himself trotted out the Presidential Emergency Board to prevent the UTU from striking railroads during our own contract negotiations back in 1995. Not only that, but then federal arbitrators rammed down our throats the very same contract that we had already overwhelmingly rejected. F#cking loser hypocrite.<P>To those of you who suggest that strikes have no real impact on a company, all I can say is that either your experiences are quite different than my own or that in reality you have no actual experience with the situation. I weathered a major IAM strike at Boeing back in 1989 and watched my wife go through another big one in 1995 and then the SPEEA strike just last year. To say that Boeing was unaffected is sheer ignorance. There is no other way to put it. Yes, they have clauses in their contracts allowing them to delay deliveries as a result of strikes, but their customers get real upset when it occurs. Boeing also usually ultimately winds up paying generous contract ratification bonuses on top of all the other concessions to get employees back to work. We're talking like $5,000 for my wife back in 95. And yes, the managers did what they could to keep the lines going, but once labor returned, at least the first week was spent fixing everything the managers f#cked up. Trust me, been there, done that.<P>Now I work for a major Class I railroad. The federal government uses the same Presidential Emergency Board to prevent us, the entire railroad industry, from striking. I guess they are worried about any public backlash that might result from a the huge disruption even a relatively short shutdown of the railroads would surely incur. It really sucks because we have no real bargaining power and the railroads know it. We can't even get a decent cost of living allowance and are slowly sliding backwards. This is how corporations are slowly strangling their employees these days. I can't really complain too much right now as most people would be happy to earn over 50K per year and work about 30 hours per week. But I have a long ways to go until retirement and can't help but wonder what will be left 30 years from now. That 50K per year might be minimum wage by then. I wonder how bad it is going to have to get before we just tell the government to go f#ck themselves and pull a wildcat strike anyhow. What I believe the labor movement really needs most today is some leadership with enough resolve and backbone to stand up and fight for their membership. And it's not always about money, either. The railroad industry has some serious quality of life issues that need addressing. I might have it okay, but some of my co-workers are literally worked into the ground. <P>And here at the railroad, we have clauses in a our contracts that allow us to honor other unions picket lines. So when Boeing goes on strike, union rail labor doesn't cross their lines to deliver their cars. Our management comes out and takes over to do it though. Funny thing is, they almost always wind up screwing stuff up, putting cars on the ground, tearing up equipment. It's pretty funny. It would be even better if they disciplined themselves in the same manner as they would us for making the same mistakes.||||reg||1
